lar/angular/pull/20972.patch","merged_at":"2017-12-18T20:09:23Z"},"body":"Allows a directive to use the expression passed directly to a property\r\nas a guard instead of filtering the type through a type guard function.\r\n\r\nThis more accurately matches the intent of the ngIf usage of its template\r\nenabling better type inference.\r\n\r\nMoved NgIf to using this type of guard instead of a function guard.\r\n\r\nCloses: #20967\r\n\r\n\r\n## PR Type\r\nWhat kind of change does this PR introduce?\r\n\r\n<!-- Please check the one that applies to this PR using \"x\". -->\r\n```\r\n[x] Feature\r\n```\r\n\r\n## What is the current behavior?\r\n\r\n#20967\r\n\r\nUsing the type guard mechanism introduced in e7d9cb3e4ccbd491e5bde166f6d2f292d7da9223 the following expression is not narrowed as expected.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n  <div *ngIf=\"!!person\">\r\n    {{person.name}\r\n  </div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nbecause the type guard function only infers that the expression `!!person` is true but does not further infer that implies person is not `undefined` causing `person.name` to report that `person` might be `undefined`.\r\n\r\n## What is the new behavior?\r\n\r\nThe code generated for an `*ngIf` uses the guard expression directly as the template guard instead of using a type guard function. This allows TypeScript to infer the correct types for `person` in the above example.\r\n\r\n## Does this PR introduce a breaking change?\r\n```\r\n[ ] Yes\r\n[x] No\r\n```\r\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/angular/angular/issues/20972/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/angular/angular/issues/20972/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null}},"event":"cross-referenced"},{"id":1384628262,"node_id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEzODQ2MjgyNjI=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/angular/angular/issues/events/1384628262","actor":{"login":"chuckjaz","id":372013